{"source":"us-fedreg","asOf":"2026-10-13","retrieved":"2026-10-13","note":"Legal basis of the main country-wide additional US tariff on each economy, compiled by Orbis from the Federal Register. Sector tariffs under Section 232 (steel, aluminium, copper, cars, trucks, lumber, pharmaceuticals and others) apply on top and are not shown. BEFORE (19 Feb 2026): IEEPA 'reciprocal' tariff, Executive Order 14257 of 2 Apr 2025 (90 FR 15041), as amended (EO 14326, 90 FR 37963); Canada and Mexico were excluded and instead paid IEEPA 'border' tariffs, EO 14193 and 14194 (90 FR 9113, 9117; Canada 35% from 1 Aug 2025 per EO 14325; USMCA-qualifying goods exempt); China: IEEPA (EO 14195 and EO 14257, as modified by EO 14357/14358 of 4 Nov 2025) on top of Section 301 tariffs from 2018; Brazil: IEEPA, EO 14323 of 30 Jul 2025 (40%, scope narrowed by EO 14361) plus the reciprocal tariff. Russia, Belarus, Cuba and North Korea were not listed under EO 14257. All IEEPA tariffs ended under EO 14389 of 20 Feb 2026 (91 FR 9437); CBP stopped collecting them on 24 Feb 2026 (CSMS #67834313). BRIDGE: Section 122 surcharge of 10% on all countries, Proclamation 11012 (91 FR 9339), 24 Feb to 24 Jul 2026. AFTER (13 Oct 2026): USTR Section 301 forced-labour actions, 91 FR 47318 (FR Doc. 2026-15181, 28 Jul 2026), effective 24 Jul 2026: 12.5% or 10% on all products of 60 economies (the EU counted as one), with exemptions including all Section 232 goods and, for Canada and Mexico, USMCA duty-free goods; EU, Taiwan (10%) and Japan, South Korea, Switzerland (12.5%) are charged 'net of MFN'. China also pays the 2018 Section 301 tariffs (continued, FR Doc. 2026-20510); Brazil also pays 25% under Section 301 from 22 Jul 2026 (FR Doc. 2026-14542); Canada also pays 50% under Section 338 of the Tariff Act of 1930 on some motor vehicles, dairy and alcoholic beverages from 19 Aug 2026 (Proclamations 11046-11048, 91 FR 46639-46663; scope changed by Proclamations 11056, 11061-11065). Hong Kong is listed separately 